## Code knowledge graph (graphy)

This repository has a pre-built code knowledge graph in `.graphy/`. For
structural questions, query the graph instead of scanning files — in this
order:

1. **Orient** — `graphy report --sections`, then `--section "<name>"`.
2. **Locate** — `graphy nodes --file <path>` when you know the file; the name
   directly when you know it; `graphy query "<question>"` when you know
   neither. Entities resolve by name, qualified name, node id, or file path.
3. **Expand** — `graphy neighbors <entity>` (`--rel`, `--kind`, `--depth`,
   `--direction`).
4. **Impact** — `graphy callers <entity>` before changing anything;
   `graphy uses <entity>` for the reverse; `graphy path <a> <b>` for how two
   things connect.
5. **Intent** — `graphy why <entity>` for the WHY:/DECISION: comments and ADRs
   attached to it. Read this before changing unfamiliar code.
6. **Read** — `graphy concept <entity>` gives the wiki concept documenting it
   (`okfw search --node-id` goes the other way).

Edges are tagged EXTRACTED (trust it), INFERRED (usually right) or AMBIGUOUS
(verify). Results are capped: `truncated` or `… N more` means you did not see
everything — raise `--limit`/`"limit"` or narrow, rather than re-asking. This
includes `graphy query`/`graphy_query`. Test entities are excluded unless you
pass `--tests`/`"tests":true`.

After editing code, run `graphy update` (incremental, sub-second) to keep the
graph fresh. `graphy mcp` serves all of this as MCP stdio tools, named
`graphy_*`; over MCP, `graphy uses` is `graphy_neighbors` with
`"direction":"out"`, and edges in `graphy_query`/`graphy_neighbors` index into
the response's `nodes` array by position.
